.TH CHMAP_CONSTRUCT_MP 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
chmap_construct_mp \- declare and create a hash map that takes its memory from a custom allocator
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chashmap.h>

chmap_construct_mp(hm_name, key_t, val_t, mmgmt_procs);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a hash map handle named hm_name. It also declares the companion
type variables. These variables carry key_t and val_t to every later
type-inferred macro. The macro then creates a map that takes all of its memory
from mmgmt_procs.

This macro is chmap_construct(3) plus an allocator. The map uses its own
hashing for key_t. chmap_construct_ch(3) replaces the hashing instead, and
chmap_construct_full(3) takes both. chmap_construct_mp_scoped(3) is the
same macro with a cleanup attribute that destroys the map at scope exit.

chmap_declare(3) and then chmap_init_mp(3) is the two-step form. Use the
two-step form when the caller must handle a creation failure instead of
the failure being fatal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name      Name for the map handle to declare
key_t        Key type; it tells every later macro how to read a key and,
             with val_t, which implementation strategy the map uses
val_t        Value type
mmgmt_procs  Pointer to a ccol_memmgmt_procs_t, or NULL for the default
             allocator
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
All four members of ccol_memmgmt_procs_t must be non-NULL
The macro copies the procedures into the map. The caller's own
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t therefore need not outlive the call. The procedures
themselves must stay callable for the whole life of the map
Everything that the map allocates later comes from these procedures, its
bucket array included. Everything that the map frees goes back to them
Uses CCOL_DEFAULT_INITIAL_BUCKET_ARRAY_SIZE (16) buckets
chmap_destroy(3) destroys the map. The companion type variables follow the
scope of the handle, so a function that gets the handle needs
chmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ccol_memmgmt_procs_t procs = {
    .malloc = my_malloc, .calloc = my_calloc,
    .realloc = my_realloc, .free = my_free,
};

chmap_construct_mp(scores, char *, int, &procs);
int score = 42;
chmap_insert(scores, "alice", score);
chmap_destroy(scores);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chmap_construct (3),
.BR chmap_construct_mp_scoped (3),
.BR chmap_construct_ch (3),
.BR chmap_construct_full (3),
.BR chmap_init_mp (3),
.BR chmap_declare (3),
.BR chmap_redeclare (3),
.BR chmap_create_full (3),
.BR chmap_destroy (3)
